    {
      "id": "01",
      "title": "Entry and exit times",
      "question": "What do DFS entry and exit times tell you about the edges of a directed graph?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Depth-first search, question one. What do entry and exit times tell you about the edges of a directed graph? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "What do <em>entry and exit times</em> tell you?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "Run DFS from zero, with a clock that ticks on every entry, and every exit. Enter zero at time zero, then one at time one, then two at time two. Two has an edge back to zero. Zero was entered, but hasn't exited: it's still on the current path. That's a back edge, and it means a cycle. Two has nothing else, so it exits at three, and one exits at four. Back at zero, its edge to two finds a node that's finished, and was entered after zero: a forward edge, a shortcut down the tree. Then enter three, at five. Its edge to two finds a finished node, entered before three: a cross edge, into a branch that's already done. Three exits at six, and zero at seven.",
