      "id": "03",
      "title": "Memoized DFS",
      "question": "How do you find the longest increasing path in a matrix?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question three. How do you find the longest strictly increasing path in a matrix, moving up, down, left, or right? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "Longest <em>increasing path</em> in a matrix?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "Treat each cell as a node, with an edge to every neighbor holding a bigger value. Values strictly increase along each edge, so there are no cycles: it's a DAG, and DFS can't loop. The longest path from a cell is one, plus the longest path from its best bigger neighbor. That never changes, so cache it. The nines and the eight have no bigger neighbor: their paths have length one. The four, and both sixes, step to one of those: two. The bottom-left two steps up to a six: three. The middle one steps left, to the two: four. The last corner steps up to the eight: two. Each cell is computed once, so the whole search is O of m times n. The answer is four: one, two, six, nine.",
